// Bun Snapshot v1, https://bun.sh/docs/test/snapshots

exports[`platform scaffold GitHub secrets mode the default renders exactly the Environment scaffold 1`] = `
"name: platform

# Deploys the acme account's fabrika BASE: IAM (Stage 1, root authz) → Operations (Stage 2) →
# runner + control plane (Stage 3). THIS repo is the per-account root of trust, so the control plane
# never deploys itself.
# Idempotent: re-run for a first bring-up OR a routine redeploy. Concrete apps deploy through fabrika.
#
# Generated + maintained by \`fabrika platform init --provider=cloudflare acme\`. Secrets + variables live in the \`acme\` GitHub
# Environment (Settings → Environments → acme) and are written there by the CLI — don't hand-edit them.
#
# contember/fabrika-platform is public and checked out tokenless. The version this account runs is pinned
# in \`fabrika.ref\` (bump it and push to roll out a new base).

on:
  workflow_dispatch:
    inputs:
      retire_legacy_propustka_worker:
        description: >-
          Name of a LEGACY propustka Worker to delete BEFORE Stage 1 (one-off migration off an old
          deploy — e.g. a CF-Access-era \`<env>-propustka-worker\`). Deleting it releases its Custom
          Domain so the new env-prod propustka can claim it. Empty = skip (the normal case).
        type: string
        default: ''
  push:
    branches: [main]
    # Only a version bump (or a workflow edit) redeploys — not a README tweak.
    paths: ['fabrika.ref', '.github/workflows/platform.yml']

concurrency:
  group: platform-deploy
  cancel-in-progress: false

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    timeout-minutes: 25
    # Secrets + variables resolve from this GitHub Environment (created + populated by \`fabrika platform init\`).
    environment: acme
    # Cloudflare creds at the JOB level so EVERY oblaka/wrangler step (provision, migrate, deploy, secret
    # put) inherits them — wrangler errors in a non-interactive shell without CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN.
    env:
      CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
      CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
      # The resource names this installation was created with (\`fabrika\` from init; unset on an older installation,
      # which keeps the legacy names). Every stage names its Workers, databases, buckets and queues by it.
      FABRIKA_CLOUDFLARE_NAMES: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_CLOUDFLARE_NAMES }}
    steps:
      - name: Checkout this repo (base config + pinned refs)
        uses: actions/checkout@v5

      - name: Resolve pinned refs
        id: ref
        run: echo "fabrika=$(tr -d '[:space:]' < fabrika.ref)" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"

      - name: Checkout contember/fabrika-platform@\${{ steps.ref.outputs.fabrika }}
        uses: actions/checkout@v5
        with:
          repository: contember/fabrika-platform
          ref: \${{ steps.ref.outputs.fabrika }}
          path: fabrika-platform

      - name: Resolve the Bun release of the pinned tag
        id: bun
        working-directory: fabrika-platform
        env:
          PINNED_REF: \${{ steps.ref.outputs.fabrika }}
        # Exactly the Bun the tag was built and released with, never \`latest\`: a newer Bun rewrites the
        # lockfile. Tags up to v0.4.3 carry no \`.bun-version\` and were all released with Bun 1.3.14.
        run: |
          if [ -f .bun-version ]; then
            version="$(tr -d '[:space:]' < .bun-version)"
          else
            version=1.3.14
          fi
          if ! printf '%s' "$version" | grep -Eq '^[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+$'; then
            echo "fabrika-platform@$PINNED_REF names no exact Bun release in .bun-version: '$version'." >&2
            exit 1
          fi
          echo "version=$version" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"

      - uses: oven-sh/setup-bun@v2
        with:
          bun-version: \${{ steps.bun.outputs.version }}
      # wrangler launches under Node even via Bun and needs Node 22+.
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v5
        with:
          node-version: '24'

      - name: Install fabrika
        working-directory: fabrika-platform
        run: bun install --frozen-lockfile

      # The Cloudflare provider spawns a BARE \`wrangler\` (Bun.spawn), so the workspace bins must be on PATH.
      - name: Put fabrika workspace bins on PATH
        working-directory: fabrika-platform
        # \`fabrika\` itself is asserted rather than assumed: \`bun install\` links a workspace package's bin
        # only when the root package depends on it, so an older pinned tag can install cleanly and still
        # have no \`fabrika\` at all — a bare \`command not found\` several steps later, naming nothing.
        run: |
          if [ ! -x node_modules/.bin/fabrika ]; then
            echo "fabrika-platform@\${{ steps.ref.outputs.fabrika }} installed no \\\`fabrika\\\` binary." >&2
            echo "Pin a tag whose root package.json depends on @fabrika/cli." >&2
            exit 1
          fi
          echo "$PWD/node_modules/.bin" >> "$GITHUB_PATH"
          echo "$PWD/packages/control/node_modules/.bin" >> "$GITHUB_PATH"
          echo "$PWD/packages/runner-cloudflare/node_modules/.bin" >> "$GITHUB_PATH"

      # ── Migration escape hatch — retire a legacy propustka Worker ─────────────────────────────────────
      # Off by default. When \`retire_legacy_propustka_worker\` is set, delete that Worker so its Custom Domain
      # is freed for the new env-prod propustka below. Its D1 database is NOT deleted (a Worker delete leaves
      # databases) — orphaned, prune it by hand later. One-off: clear the input on subsequent runs.
      - name: Retire legacy propustka Worker
        if: \${{ inputs.retire_legacy_propustka_worker != '' }}
        env:
          CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
          LEGACY: \${{ inputs.retire_legacy_propustka_worker }}
        # \`wrangler delete\` is non-interactive in CI (no TTY → no confirmation prompt). Removing the Worker
        # detaches its Custom Domain. If the domain attach below races a just-freed hostname, re-run (idempotent).
        run: bunx wrangler delete --name "$LEGACY"

      # ── Stage 1 — IAM (root authz) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      # IAM is a regular Cloudflare provider bundle. It declares no schema: its keys sync after its Worker deploys,
      # so IAM cannot authenticate a reconcile in its own stage.
      - name: Deploy IAM
        working-directory: fabrika-platform/packages/iam
        env:
          CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_HOSTNAME }}
          # Non-secret config (Environment Variables).
          FABRIKA_IAM_HUMAN_EMAIL_DOMAINS: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_HUMAN_EMAIL_DOMAINS }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_HUMAN_EMAILS: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_HUMAN_EMAILS }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_BOOTSTRAP_ADMINS: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_BOOTSTRAP_ADMINS }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_ENABLED: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_ENABLED }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_PASSWORD_ENABLED: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_PASSWORD_ENABLED }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_ISSUER: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_ISSUER }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_CLIENT_ID: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_CLIENT_ID }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_SCOPES: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_SCOPES }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_REQUIRE_VERIFIED_EMAIL: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_REQUIRE_VERIFIED_EMAIL }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_PROVIDER: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_PROVIDER }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM_NAME: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM_NAME }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_SIGNING_KEYS: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_IAM_SIGNING_KEYS }}
          # Seals each administrator's TOTP secret; without it IAM offers no TOTP enrolment.
          FABRIKA_IAM_DATA_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_IAM_DATA_KEY }}
          OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_PROVISIONING_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_IAM_PROVISIONING_KEY }}
          # Control's public origin: IAM hands its mail to Control's relay there, under a key derived from the
          # provisioning key, and the relay sends it with Control's copy of the transport.
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_URL: https://\${{ vars.FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN }}
          # The operator's identity-bound external OAuth clients (ADR-0084), a JSON array; unset registers none.
          FABRIKA_PLATFORM_MCP_CLIENTS_JSON: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_PLATFORM_MCP_CLIENTS_JSON }}
          # Only so an identity resource on the Operations host is refused here, before IAM deploys.
          OPERATIONS_HOSTNAME: \${{ vars.OPERATIONS_HOSTNAME }}
          # Every built-in mail provider's variables, written by init from the provider registry: IAM reads only the
          # selected provider's, and an unset one is ''.
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_BROADCAST_FROM: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_BROADCAST_FROM }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_BREVO_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_BREVO_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_PROJECT_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_PROJECT_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_BROADCAST_ROUTE: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_BROADCAST_ROUTE }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_BROADCAST_STREAM: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_BROADCAST_STREAM }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_RESEND_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_RESEND_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_TAG: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_TAG }}
        # \`platform deploy\`, not \`app deploy\`: IAM's own Worker is a PLATFORM resource name, and only the
        # platform verb may name one. The single-component form deploys just this config.
        run: fabrika platform deploy --provider=cloudflare --env=prod --component-config=fabrika.config.ts

      # ── Stage 2 — Operations ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      # Operations must exist before control: control carries a private service binding to it.
      - name: Deploy Operations
        working-directory: fabrika-platform/packages/operations
        env:
          CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
          # The provider's app-deploy adapter reads FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN as the selected app domain.
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN: \${{ vars.OPERATIONS_HOSTNAME }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_ISSUER: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_ISSUER }}
          # The console's origin: the command-line client's Operations resource is a path on it (ADR-0049).
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_URL: https://\${{ vars.FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN }}
          OPERATIONS_SYNC_KEY: \${{ secrets.OPERATIONS_SYNC_KEY }}
        run: fabrika platform deploy --provider=cloudflare --env=prod --component-config=fabrika.config.ts

      # ── Stage 3 — fabrika platform (executor + control plane) ────────────────────────────────────────
      - name: Deploy fabrika runner + control plane
        id: platform
        working-directory: fabrika-platform
        env:
          CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_VAULT_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_CONTROL_VAULT_KEY }}
          # The seeded IAM provisioning key — this stage registers the console's schema, with the console's return
          # origin, IAM's own vocabulary and the external applications' roles with it (Bearer px_), at FABRIKA_IAM_ISSUER.
          FABRIKA_IAM_PROVISIONING_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_IAM_PROVISIONING_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_RETURN_ORIGINS: https://\${{ vars.FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN }}
          OPERATIONS_SYNC_KEY: \${{ secrets.OPERATIONS_SYNC_KEY }}
          # \`wrangler secret put FABRIKA_RUNNER_KEY\` on BOTH Workers this step deploys: the control plane
          # presents it to the deploy runner, and the runner rejects an RPC caller without it. One step,
          # one value, because a mismatched pair breaks every deploy.
          FABRIKA_RUNNER_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_RUNNER_KEY }}
          # Non-secret config (Environment Variables).
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_ISSUER: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_ISSUER }}
          OPERATIONS_ARTIFACT_ORIGIN: https://\${{ vars.OPERATIONS_HOSTNAME }}
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_BOOTSTRAP_ADMINS: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_CONTROL_BOOTSTRAP_ADMINS }}
          # The same client list IAM was deployed with: this stage reconciles each external application's roles into IAM.
          FABRIKA_PLATFORM_MCP_CLIENTS_JSON: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_PLATFORM_MCP_CLIENTS_JSON }}
          # The installation's mail transport, the same set as IAM's: the control plane hands it to every application
          # whose job declares \`mailer: 'installation'\`. Its credentials become Worker secrets of the control plane only.
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_PROVIDER: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_PROVIDER }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM_NAME: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM_NAME }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_BROADCAST_FROM: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_BROADCAST_FROM }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_BREVO_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_BREVO_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_PROJECT_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_PROJECT_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_BROADCAST_ROUTE: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_BROADCAST_ROUTE }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_BROADCAST_STREAM: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_BROADCAST_STREAM }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_RESEND_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_RESEND_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_TAG: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_TAG }}
        run: |
          fabrika platform deploy --provider=cloudflare \\
            --runner-config=packages/runner-cloudflare/fabrika-runner.config.ts \\
            --worker-config=packages/control/fabrika.config.ts

      - name: Summary
        if: always()
        run: |
          {
            echo "### platform deploy → acme"
            echo ""
            echo "- fabrika ref: \\\`\${{ steps.ref.outputs.fabrika || '(unresolved)' }}\\\`"
            echo "- platform deploy: \\\`\${{ steps.platform.outcome || 'not run' }}\\\`"
            echo "- runner image source: account-local build from the pinned fabrika checkout"
            echo "- retired legacy propustka: \\\`\${{ inputs.retire_legacy_propustka_worker || '(none)' }}\\\`"
          } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
"
`;

exports[`platform scaffold GitHub secrets mode the default renders exactly the Environment scaffold 2`] = `
"# fabrika-platform (acme)

The **per-account root of trust** for the acme Cloudflare account. Its GitHub pipeline deploys the
fabrika control-plane base so the control plane never deploys itself:

\`\`\`
Stage 1 — IAM (root authz)
Stage 2 — Operations
Stage 3 — fabrika runner + control plane
apps (crm, notes, …)                    ← deployed through fabrika, not here
\`\`\`

Generated and maintained by **\`fabrika platform init --provider=cloudflare acme\`** (\`@fabrika/cli\`). Secrets and variables live in
the **\`acme\` GitHub Environment** (Settings → Environments) and are written there by the CLI.
Scaffold commits use \`[skip ci]\`; init explicitly dispatches the workflow only after that Environment is configured.

Every credential this installation has is in that Environment — the Cloudflare API token, the vault KEK,
IAM's signing and data keys, the provisioning key and \`FABRIKA_RUNNER_KEY\` (the shared key the
control plane presents to the deploy runner; Stage 3 pushes it to both Workers). Init therefore protects
\`main\` (no force pushes, no deletions) on a repository it creates, and restricts the Environment to
protected branches. The two go together: the Environment policy admits any branch that carries no
protection rule, so it is worth nothing on its own. GitHub Free offers neither on a private repository —
if init warned that one was refused, run the command it printed once the plan allows it.

Whatever the plan, add yourself as a **required reviewer** on the \`acme\` Environment
(Settings → Environments → acme → Required reviewers). It is the only rule that stops a push to
\`main\` from spending these credentials without a human first agreeing to it. Init deliberately does not
set it: it turns every deploy into an approval click, which is the operator's call and not the CLI's.

\`contember/fabrika-platform\` is public and pinned in [\`fabrika.ref\`](./fabrika.ref). The pipeline checks it
out once, then runs \`fabrika platform deploy --provider=cloudflare\` three times: \`--component-config\` for
IAM, \`--component-config\` for Operations, and the \`--runner-config\`/\`--worker-config\` pair for
\`packages/runner-cloudflare\` and \`packages/control\`. Every stage is a PLATFORM deploy because each names a
platform resource; \`fabrika app deploy\` is for applications and is refused one. The workflow builds the runner image from that same checkout into this account's
registry. It never depends on an image published by upstream CI or another account. Re-running it is
a safe redeploy.

## First bring-up

Run init with the Cloudflare API token in \`CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN\`:

\`\`\`sh
fabrika platform init --provider=cloudflare acme \\
  --console-host=<host> --operations-host=<host> --iam-host=<host> \\
  --auth-methods=<oidc|password|oidc,password> --email-provider=<none|ahasend|brevo|cloudflare|lettermint|postmark|resend|smartemailing>
\`\`\`

\`fabrika platform init --provider=cloudflare --help\` lists the optional flags. It creates this repo,
the Environment with its secrets and variables, and triggers the workflow. The run builds and pushes the
runner container image into the account.

Public repositories deploy without a GitHub App. To deploy private repositories, connect GitHub from the
control plane at **Settings → Source**: each connection is a GitHub App owned by an organization or a personal account, whose key
Control keeps in its encrypted vault, never in this Environment.

To close the escape hatch once IAM grants you admin, set the \`acme\` Environment variable
\`FABRIKA_CONTROL_BOOTSTRAP_ADMINS\` to \`[]\` and re-run the workflow.

IAM authentication methods are independent Environment switches: \`FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_ENABLED\` and
\`FABRIKA_IAM_PASSWORD_ENABLED\`. At least one must be \`true\`. Password enrollment is managed through
IAM provisioning and its admin surface. Email delivery is optional; \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_PROVIDER=none\`
keeps password enrollment and reset admin-driven. A provider also needs the sender variable
\`FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM\` (optionally \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM_NAME\`) and its own Environment secrets and variables:

- \`ahasend\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_API_KEY\` (secret), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_ACCOUNT_ID\` (variable), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_BROADCAST_FROM\` (variable, optional)
- \`brevo\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_BREVO_API_KEY\` (secret)
- \`cloudflare\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN\` (secret), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID\` (variable)
- \`lettermint\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_PROJECT_TOKEN\` (secret), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_BROADCAST_ROUTE\` (variable, optional)
- \`postmark\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN\` (secret), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_BROADCAST_STREAM\` (variable, optional)
- \`resend\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_RESEND_API_KEY\` (secret)
- \`smartemailing\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME\` (secret), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY\` (secret), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_TAG\` (variable, optional)

The workflow hands the same set to IAM and to the control plane. The control plane hands it to every application
whose job declares \`mailer: 'installation'\`, at that application's deploy.

To change or rotate them, re-run \`fabrika platform init\` with the new values; it rewrites this Environment
and triggers the workflow, which redeploys IAM and the control plane. An application gets the new set at its next
deploy: redeploy each environment that sends installation mail with
\`fabrika control deploy --app=<id> --env=<name>\`, or **Deploy** in the console.

Switching provider leaves the old provider's values stored: init writes the new ones and deletes none. IAM and
the control plane read only the selected provider's variables, but delete the old credentials so nothing holds them, for
example after leaving SmartEmailing:

\`\`\`sh
gh secret delete FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME --env acme
gh secret delete FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY --env acme
gh variable delete FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_TAG --env acme
\`\`\`

Remove the same names from the installer env file (init prints its path when it starts), or a later run of
init resumes them.
IAM's and the control plane's Workers also keep the old provider's credentials as Worker secrets, which neither reads.
Delete each of them from both Workers, with the account's id in \`CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID\` and its API token in
\`CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN\`:

\`\`\`sh
for worker in prod-propustka-worker prod-vozka; do
  bunx wrangler secret delete FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME --name "$worker"
  bunx wrangler secret delete FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY --name "$worker"
done
\`\`\`

## Routine redeploy or version bump

- Bump [\`fabrika.ref\`](./fabrika.ref) to a new \`contember/fabrika-platform\` commit or tag and push.
- Or run _Actions → platform → Run workflow_ manually.

Both paths rebuild the account-owned runner image from the exact checkout selected by \`fabrika.ref\`.

\`fabrika.ref\` is operator-owned. \`fabrika platform init\` creates it once and does not overwrite later version pins.
A bump does not rewrite this workflow. Re-run \`fabrika platform init --provider=cloudflare acme\` with the same
flags, with the CLI of the release \`fabrika.ref\` names, to bring \`.github/workflows/platform.yml\` to the one that release
generates. A change in what the workflow hands a stage (such as the mail transport the control plane holds) reaches
the account only this way.
"
`;

exports[`platform scaffold GitHub secrets mode repository secrets render a workflow without \`environment:\` that reads the same secrets and variables 1`] = `
"name: platform

# Deploys the acme account's fabrika BASE: IAM (Stage 1, root authz) → Operations (Stage 2) →
# runner + control plane (Stage 3). THIS repo is the per-account root of trust, so the control plane
# never deploys itself.
# Idempotent: re-run for a first bring-up OR a routine redeploy. Concrete apps deploy through fabrika.
#
# Generated + maintained by \`fabrika platform init --provider=cloudflare acme --github-secrets=repository\`. Secrets + variables
# are this repository's own (Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions) and are written there by the CLI — don't
# hand-edit them. There is no GitHub Environment, so no deployment protection rule guards them: any workflow of this
# repository, on any branch, can read them.
#
# contember/fabrika-platform is public and checked out tokenless. The version this account runs is pinned
# in \`fabrika.ref\` (bump it and push to roll out a new base).

on:
  workflow_dispatch:
    inputs:
      retire_legacy_propustka_worker:
        description: >-
          Name of a LEGACY propustka Worker to delete BEFORE Stage 1 (one-off migration off an old
          deploy — e.g. a CF-Access-era \`<env>-propustka-worker\`). Deleting it releases its Custom
          Domain so the new env-prod propustka can claim it. Empty = skip (the normal case).
        type: string
        default: ''
  push:
    branches: [main]
    # Only a version bump (or a workflow edit) redeploys — not a README tweak.
    paths: ['fabrika.ref', '.github/workflows/platform.yml']

concurrency:
  group: platform-deploy
  cancel-in-progress: false

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    timeout-minutes: 25
    # Secrets + variables resolve from this repository's own (populated by \`fabrika platform init\`); no Environment.
    # Cloudflare creds at the JOB level so EVERY oblaka/wrangler step (provision, migrate, deploy, secret
    # put) inherits them — wrangler errors in a non-interactive shell without CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN.
    env:
      CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
      CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
      # The resource names this installation was created with (\`fabrika\` from init; unset on an older installation,
      # which keeps the legacy names). Every stage names its Workers, databases, buckets and queues by it.
      FABRIKA_CLOUDFLARE_NAMES: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_CLOUDFLARE_NAMES }}
    steps:
      - name: Checkout this repo (base config + pinned refs)
        uses: actions/checkout@v5

      - name: Resolve pinned refs
        id: ref
        run: echo "fabrika=$(tr -d '[:space:]' < fabrika.ref)" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"

      - name: Checkout contember/fabrika-platform@\${{ steps.ref.outputs.fabrika }}
        uses: actions/checkout@v5
        with:
          repository: contember/fabrika-platform
          ref: \${{ steps.ref.outputs.fabrika }}
          path: fabrika-platform

      - name: Resolve the Bun release of the pinned tag
        id: bun
        working-directory: fabrika-platform
        env:
          PINNED_REF: \${{ steps.ref.outputs.fabrika }}
        # Exactly the Bun the tag was built and released with, never \`latest\`: a newer Bun rewrites the
        # lockfile. Tags up to v0.4.3 carry no \`.bun-version\` and were all released with Bun 1.3.14.
        run: |
          if [ -f .bun-version ]; then
            version="$(tr -d '[:space:]' < .bun-version)"
          else
            version=1.3.14
          fi
          if ! printf '%s' "$version" | grep -Eq '^[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+$'; then
            echo "fabrika-platform@$PINNED_REF names no exact Bun release in .bun-version: '$version'." >&2
            exit 1
          fi
          echo "version=$version" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"

      - uses: oven-sh/setup-bun@v2
        with:
          bun-version: \${{ steps.bun.outputs.version }}
      # wrangler launches under Node even via Bun and needs Node 22+.
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v5
        with:
          node-version: '24'

      - name: Install fabrika
        working-directory: fabrika-platform
        run: bun install --frozen-lockfile

      # The Cloudflare provider spawns a BARE \`wrangler\` (Bun.spawn), so the workspace bins must be on PATH.
      - name: Put fabrika workspace bins on PATH
        working-directory: fabrika-platform
        # \`fabrika\` itself is asserted rather than assumed: \`bun install\` links a workspace package's bin
        # only when the root package depends on it, so an older pinned tag can install cleanly and still
        # have no \`fabrika\` at all — a bare \`command not found\` several steps later, naming nothing.
        run: |
          if [ ! -x node_modules/.bin/fabrika ]; then
            echo "fabrika-platform@\${{ steps.ref.outputs.fabrika }} installed no \\\`fabrika\\\` binary." >&2
            echo "Pin a tag whose root package.json depends on @fabrika/cli." >&2
            exit 1
          fi
          echo "$PWD/node_modules/.bin" >> "$GITHUB_PATH"
          echo "$PWD/packages/control/node_modules/.bin" >> "$GITHUB_PATH"
          echo "$PWD/packages/runner-cloudflare/node_modules/.bin" >> "$GITHUB_PATH"

      # ── Migration escape hatch — retire a legacy propustka Worker ─────────────────────────────────────
      # Off by default. When \`retire_legacy_propustka_worker\` is set, delete that Worker so its Custom Domain
      # is freed for the new env-prod propustka below. Its D1 database is NOT deleted (a Worker delete leaves
      # databases) — orphaned, prune it by hand later. One-off: clear the input on subsequent runs.
      - name: Retire legacy propustka Worker
        if: \${{ inputs.retire_legacy_propustka_worker != '' }}
        env:
          CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
          LEGACY: \${{ inputs.retire_legacy_propustka_worker }}
        # \`wrangler delete\` is non-interactive in CI (no TTY → no confirmation prompt). Removing the Worker
        # detaches its Custom Domain. If the domain attach below races a just-freed hostname, re-run (idempotent).
        run: bunx wrangler delete --name "$LEGACY"

      # ── Stage 1 — IAM (root authz) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      # IAM is a regular Cloudflare provider bundle. It declares no schema: its keys sync after its Worker deploys,
      # so IAM cannot authenticate a reconcile in its own stage.
      - name: Deploy IAM
        working-directory: fabrika-platform/packages/iam
        env:
          CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_HOSTNAME }}
          # Non-secret config (Environment Variables).
          FABRIKA_IAM_HUMAN_EMAIL_DOMAINS: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_HUMAN_EMAIL_DOMAINS }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_HUMAN_EMAILS: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_HUMAN_EMAILS }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_BOOTSTRAP_ADMINS: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_BOOTSTRAP_ADMINS }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_ENABLED: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_ENABLED }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_PASSWORD_ENABLED: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_PASSWORD_ENABLED }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_ISSUER: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_ISSUER }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_CLIENT_ID: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_CLIENT_ID }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_SCOPES: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_SCOPES }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_REQUIRE_VERIFIED_EMAIL: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_REQUIRE_VERIFIED_EMAIL }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_PROVIDER: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_PROVIDER }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM_NAME: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM_NAME }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_SIGNING_KEYS: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_IAM_SIGNING_KEYS }}
          # Seals each administrator's TOTP secret; without it IAM offers no TOTP enrolment.
          FABRIKA_IAM_DATA_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_IAM_DATA_KEY }}
          OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_PROVISIONING_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_IAM_PROVISIONING_KEY }}
          # Control's public origin: IAM hands its mail to Control's relay there, under a key derived from the
          # provisioning key, and the relay sends it with Control's copy of the transport.
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_URL: https://\${{ vars.FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN }}
          # The operator's identity-bound external OAuth clients (ADR-0084), a JSON array; unset registers none.
          FABRIKA_PLATFORM_MCP_CLIENTS_JSON: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_PLATFORM_MCP_CLIENTS_JSON }}
          # Only so an identity resource on the Operations host is refused here, before IAM deploys.
          OPERATIONS_HOSTNAME: \${{ vars.OPERATIONS_HOSTNAME }}
          # Every built-in mail provider's variables, written by init from the provider registry: IAM reads only the
          # selected provider's, and an unset one is ''.
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_BROADCAST_FROM: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_BROADCAST_FROM }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_BREVO_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_BREVO_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_PROJECT_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_PROJECT_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_BROADCAST_ROUTE: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_BROADCAST_ROUTE }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_BROADCAST_STREAM: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_BROADCAST_STREAM }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_RESEND_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_RESEND_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_TAG: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_TAG }}
        # \`platform deploy\`, not \`app deploy\`: IAM's own Worker is a PLATFORM resource name, and only the
        # platform verb may name one. The single-component form deploys just this config.
        run: fabrika platform deploy --provider=cloudflare --env=prod --component-config=fabrika.config.ts

      # ── Stage 2 — Operations ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      # Operations must exist before control: control carries a private service binding to it.
      - name: Deploy Operations
        working-directory: fabrika-platform/packages/operations
        env:
          CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
          # The provider's app-deploy adapter reads FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN as the selected app domain.
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN: \${{ vars.OPERATIONS_HOSTNAME }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_ISSUER: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_ISSUER }}
          # The console's origin: the command-line client's Operations resource is a path on it (ADR-0049).
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_URL: https://\${{ vars.FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN }}
          OPERATIONS_SYNC_KEY: \${{ secrets.OPERATIONS_SYNC_KEY }}
        run: fabrika platform deploy --provider=cloudflare --env=prod --component-config=fabrika.config.ts

      # ── Stage 3 — fabrika platform (executor + control plane) ────────────────────────────────────────
      - name: Deploy fabrika runner + control plane
        id: platform
        working-directory: fabrika-platform
        env:
          CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_VAULT_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_CONTROL_VAULT_KEY }}
          # The seeded IAM provisioning key — this stage registers the console's schema, with the console's return
          # origin, IAM's own vocabulary and the external applications' roles with it (Bearer px_), at FABRIKA_IAM_ISSUER.
          FABRIKA_IAM_PROVISIONING_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_IAM_PROVISIONING_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_RETURN_ORIGINS: https://\${{ vars.FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN }}
          OPERATIONS_SYNC_KEY: \${{ secrets.OPERATIONS_SYNC_KEY }}
          # \`wrangler secret put FABRIKA_RUNNER_KEY\` on BOTH Workers this step deploys: the control plane
          # presents it to the deploy runner, and the runner rejects an RPC caller without it. One step,
          # one value, because a mismatched pair breaks every deploy.
          FABRIKA_RUNNER_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_RUNNER_KEY }}
          # Non-secret config (Environment Variables).
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_CONTROL_DOMAIN }}
          FABRIKA_IAM_ISSUER: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_IAM_ISSUER }}
          OPERATIONS_ARTIFACT_ORIGIN: https://\${{ vars.OPERATIONS_HOSTNAME }}
          FABRIKA_CONTROL_BOOTSTRAP_ADMINS: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_CONTROL_BOOTSTRAP_ADMINS }}
          # The same client list IAM was deployed with: this stage reconciles each external application's roles into IAM.
          FABRIKA_PLATFORM_MCP_CLIENTS_JSON: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_PLATFORM_MCP_CLIENTS_JSON }}
          # The installation's mail transport, the same set as IAM's: the control plane hands it to every application
          # whose job declares \`mailer: 'installation'\`. Its credentials become Worker secrets of the control plane only.
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_PROVIDER: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_PROVIDER }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM_NAME: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM_NAME }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_BROADCAST_FROM: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_BROADCAST_FROM }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_BREVO_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_BREVO_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_PROJECT_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_PROJECT_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_BROADCAST_ROUTE: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_BROADCAST_ROUTE }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_BROADCAST_STREAM: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_BROADCAST_STREAM }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_RESEND_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_RESEND_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY: \${{ secrets.FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY }}
          FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_TAG: \${{ vars.FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_TAG }}
        run: |
          fabrika platform deploy --provider=cloudflare \\
            --runner-config=packages/runner-cloudflare/fabrika-runner.config.ts \\
            --worker-config=packages/control/fabrika.config.ts

      - name: Summary
        if: always()
        run: |
          {
            echo "### platform deploy → acme"
            echo ""
            echo "- fabrika ref: \\\`\${{ steps.ref.outputs.fabrika || '(unresolved)' }}\\\`"
            echo "- platform deploy: \\\`\${{ steps.platform.outcome || 'not run' }}\\\`"
            echo "- runner image source: account-local build from the pinned fabrika checkout"
            echo "- retired legacy propustka: \\\`\${{ inputs.retire_legacy_propustka_worker || '(none)' }}\\\`"
          } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
"
`;

exports[`platform scaffold GitHub secrets mode the repository README states the trade-off and names no Environment command 1`] = `
"# fabrika-platform (acme)

The **per-account root of trust** for the acme Cloudflare account. Its GitHub pipeline deploys the
fabrika control-plane base so the control plane never deploys itself:

\`\`\`
Stage 1 — IAM (root authz)
Stage 2 — Operations
Stage 3 — fabrika runner + control plane
apps (crm, notes, …)                    ← deployed through fabrika, not here
\`\`\`

Generated and maintained by **\`fabrika platform init --provider=cloudflare acme --github-secrets=repository\`** (\`@fabrika/cli\`).
Secrets and variables are **this repository's own Actions secrets and variables** (Settings → Secrets and variables →
Actions) and are written there by the CLI. There is no GitHub Environment: this installation was made for a plan whose
private repositories have none (GitHub Free organizations).
Scaffold commits use \`[skip ci]\`; init explicitly dispatches the workflow only after those secrets are written.

Every credential this installation has is a secret of this repository — the Cloudflare API token, the vault KEK,
IAM's signing key, the provisioning key and \`FABRIKA_RUNNER_KEY\` (the shared key the
control plane presents to the deploy runner; Stage 3 pushes it to both Workers).

**The trade-off.** Without an Environment there are no deployment protection rules: no deployment-branch policy, no
required reviewer, no wait timer. Any workflow in this repository, on any branch, can read every one of these secrets,
and anyone who can push a branch can add such a workflow. So nobody may have write access to this repository who is
not trusted with every credential of the installation. Init protects \`main\` (no force pushes, no deletions) on
a repository it creates where the plan allows it; that does not stop a workflow on another branch from reading the
secrets. To get the protection rules, move the repository to a plan that offers Environments on private repositories
and re-install with \`--github-secrets=environment\` (init refuses to keep both copies; it says what to delete first).

\`contember/fabrika-platform\` is public and pinned in [\`fabrika.ref\`](./fabrika.ref). The pipeline checks it
out once, then runs \`fabrika platform deploy --provider=cloudflare\` three times: \`--component-config\` for
IAM, \`--component-config\` for Operations, and the \`--runner-config\`/\`--worker-config\` pair for
\`packages/runner-cloudflare\` and \`packages/control\`. Every stage is a PLATFORM deploy because each names a
platform resource; \`fabrika app deploy\` is for applications and is refused one. The workflow builds the runner image from that same checkout into this account's
registry. It never depends on an image published by upstream CI or another account. Re-running it is
a safe redeploy.

## First bring-up

Run init with the Cloudflare API token in \`CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN\`:

\`\`\`sh
fabrika platform init --provider=cloudflare acme \\
  --console-host=<host> --operations-host=<host> --iam-host=<host> \\
  --auth-methods=<oidc|password|oidc,password> --email-provider=<none|ahasend|brevo|cloudflare|lettermint|postmark|resend|smartemailing>
\`\`\`

Add \`--github-secrets=repository\` to keep the secrets at the repository level, as this installation does.
\`fabrika platform init --provider=cloudflare --help\` lists the other optional flags. It creates this repo, writes its
secrets and variables, and triggers the workflow. The run builds and pushes the runner container image into the account.

Public repositories deploy without a GitHub App. To deploy private repositories, connect GitHub from the
control plane at **Settings → Source**: each connection is a GitHub App owned by an organization or a personal account, whose key
Control keeps in its encrypted vault, never in this repository's secrets.

To close the escape hatch once IAM grants you admin, set the repository variable
\`FABRIKA_CONTROL_BOOTSTRAP_ADMINS\` to \`[]\` and re-run the workflow.

IAM authentication methods are independent repository variables: \`FABRIKA_IAM_OIDC_ENABLED\` and
\`FABRIKA_IAM_PASSWORD_ENABLED\`. At least one must be \`true\`. Password enrollment is managed through
IAM provisioning and its admin surface. Email delivery is optional; \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_PROVIDER=none\`
keeps password enrollment and reset admin-driven. A provider also needs the sender variable
\`FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM\` (optionally \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_FROM_NAME\`) and its own repository secrets and variables:

- \`ahasend\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_API_KEY\` (secret), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_ACCOUNT_ID\` (variable), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_AHASEND_BROADCAST_FROM\` (variable, optional)
- \`brevo\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_BREVO_API_KEY\` (secret)
- \`cloudflare\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN\` (secret), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID\` (variable)
- \`lettermint\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_PROJECT_TOKEN\` (secret), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_LETTERMINT_BROADCAST_ROUTE\` (variable, optional)
- \`postmark\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN\` (secret), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_POSTMARK_BROADCAST_STREAM\` (variable, optional)
- \`resend\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_RESEND_API_KEY\` (secret)
- \`smartemailing\`: \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME\` (secret), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY\` (secret), \`FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_TAG\` (variable, optional)

The workflow hands the same set to IAM and to the control plane. The control plane hands it to every application
whose job declares \`mailer: 'installation'\`, at that application's deploy.

To change or rotate them, re-run \`fabrika platform init\` with the new values; it rewrites these repository secrets
and triggers the workflow, which redeploys IAM and the control plane. An application gets the new set at its next
deploy: redeploy each environment that sends installation mail with
\`fabrika control deploy --app=<id> --env=<name>\`, or **Deploy** in the console.

Switching provider leaves the old provider's values stored: init writes the new ones and deletes none. IAM and
the control plane read only the selected provider's variables, but delete the old credentials so nothing holds them, for
example after leaving SmartEmailing:

\`\`\`sh
gh secret delete FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME
gh secret delete FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY
gh variable delete FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_TAG
\`\`\`

Remove the same names from the installer env file (init prints its path when it starts), or a later run of
init resumes them.
IAM's and the control plane's Workers also keep the old provider's credentials as Worker secrets, which neither reads.
Delete each of them from both Workers, with the account's id in \`CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID\` and its API token in
\`CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN\`:

\`\`\`sh
for worker in prod-propustka-worker prod-vozka; do
  bunx wrangler secret delete FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_USERNAME --name "$worker"
  bunx wrangler secret delete FABRIKA_EMAIL_SMARTEMAILING_API_KEY --name "$worker"
done
\`\`\`

## Routine redeploy or version bump

- Bump [\`fabrika.ref\`](./fabrika.ref) to a new \`contember/fabrika-platform\` commit or tag and push.
- Or run _Actions → platform → Run workflow_ manually.

Both paths rebuild the account-owned runner image from the exact checkout selected by \`fabrika.ref\`.

\`fabrika.ref\` is operator-owned. \`fabrika platform init\` creates it once and does not overwrite later version pins.
A bump does not rewrite this workflow. Re-run \`fabrika platform init --provider=cloudflare acme\` with the same
flags (\`--github-secrets=repository\` among them), with the CLI of the release \`fabrika.ref\` names, to bring \`.github/workflows/platform.yml\` to the one that release
generates. A change in what the workflow hands a stage (such as the mail transport the control plane holds) reaches
the account only this way.
"
`;
